## Local setup

Before generation, complete the platform's developer onboarding checklist.
Then run the generated preflight and setup:

```bash
python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py
make check
```

Run `make help` for individual development, evaluation, and bundle targets.

Use `python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py --check-only` to verify tools,
authentication, SDK volume access, and compute-policy visibility without
installing anything. The setup never grants permissions or creates cloud
resources.

Replace every `replace-with-*` value in `aai-platform.yml` before running a
provider integration.

## How the agent works

- `src/app/tools.py` registers async code tools (plain Python) in the
  application-owned `AsyncToolRegistry`; for governed structured-data lookups,
  create Unity
  Catalog functions.
{{ if and (eq .include_serving "yes") (eq .model_provider "databricks") }}
  Ask the platform owner to grant the App service principal least-privilege
  `EXECUTE` on each function.
{{ else }}
  Declare each function with least-privilege `EXECUTE` access in the reviewed
  deployment resources you add later.
{{ end }}
- `src/app/agent.py` drives a bounded async loop over the provider-native
  client, executes async tools, and validates the final answer with the
  Pydantic contract in `src/app/schemas.py`.
- `aai-core` owns only the serializable request/response boundary; the native
  client, stream, tools, and loop remain recognizable application objects.
- Tests script provider-native responses and streams with async fakes, so they
  remain credential-free and exercise cancellation and cleanup.

## Release lifecycle

1. Edit `prompts/system_prompt.json`; register a version:
   `python scripts/register_prompt.py`. Record the returned immutable version
   and set `variables.prompt_version.default` in `databricks.yml` to that exact
   value. Do not put a mutable alias in deployment configuration.
2. Grow `evals/data/release_cases.json` — every case declares
   exact `expected_tool_calls` names and arguments (or an empty list for a
   no-tool case), so missing, repeated, mis-parameterized, or gratuitous
   calls fail the gate.
3. Configure the approved `judge-model` endpoint, then run the gate:
   `make evaluate PROMPT_VERSION=<version>` — explicitly routed
   Correctness/Safety judges plus an exact compatibility layer over MLflow
   `ToolCallCorrectness` and traced TOOL spans. The required version must be
   the same value recorded in `databricks.yml`. The gate covers empty and
   repeated-call trajectories as well as names/arguments, with thresholds in
   `evals/gate_config.json` and baseline regression. After a released run,
   append `EVALUATE_ARGS=--update-baseline`. The gate records an adopt or
   reject decision run and prints its `decision_run_id`.
   After case changes merge, register the ground truth in Unity Catalog:
   `python scripts/sync_dataset.py` — evaluation runs link the dataset
   by name.
4. Promote the prompt (`python scripts/promote_prompt.py --version <n>
   --decision-run-id <run-id> --to validation|production`). The script reloads
   and verifies the persisted decision artifact, tags, gate metrics, and exact
   prompt binding before moving either alias.
{{ if and (eq .include_serving "yes") (eq .model_provider "databricks") }}
5. Before enabling deployment, have the approved platform process create the
   Databricks App named `{{.application_name}}-dev`. App creation provisions
   its dedicated service principal. The platform owner must grant that
   identity only:

   - `CAN_EDIT` on experiment `{{.experiment_id}}`;
   - `CAN_QUERY` on serving endpoint `{{.model_deployment}}`;
   - `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `EXECUTE` on Prompt Registry function
     `{{.catalog}}.{{.schema}}.{{.prompt_name}}`; and
   - `READ VOLUME` on the SDK artifact volume represented by
     `{{.aai_core_volume}}`.

   The deploy identity also needs the externally approved rights required to
   update this App. A platform owner then performs the one-time binding:

   ```bash
   databricks bundle deployment bind \
     agent_app {{.application_name}}-dev -t dev
   ```

   `resources/agent_app.yml` deliberately contains no permission-bearing App
   resource bindings. The deploy workflow verifies that the App already
   exists before `bundle deploy`, so CI cannot become the identity or
   permissions bootstrap.

6. Deploy the bundle, run the workspace gate, and only then start or restart
   the externally provisioned Databricks App:

   ```bash
   databricks bundle deploy -t dev
   databricks bundle run release_gate -t dev
   databricks bundle run agent_app -t dev
   ```

   `src/app/endpoint.py`, `start_server.py`, and `app.yaml` expose the native
   MLflow Responses API contract. The bundle passes the same
   `${var.prompt_version}` to the `release_gate` job and to the App as
   `AAI_PROMPT_VERSION`; App startup rejects a missing, invalid, or alias-like
   value and `ToolAgent` loads only that exact version.
7. Enable production monitoring from `notebooks/02_enable_monitoring.py`
   (sampled scorers are Beta and notebook-only) and attach user feedback to
   traces; curate real failures back into the release cases.
{{ else }}
5. Serving is out of scope for this generated configuration. The bundle still
   deploys and runs `release_gate`; there is no `agent_app` resource. Route the
   model through a governed Databricks endpoint and regenerate (or add
   equivalent reviewed resources) before introducing an HTTP deployment.
{{ end }}

Pull-request CI runs only the deterministic checks
(`evals/offline_checks.py`) — it never holds credentials. The platform team
must onboard this repository (main-branch federated credential, service
principal, constrained compute, serving permissions for the deploy step,
non-secret repository variables) before the deploy workflow is enabled.

## Optional durable LangGraph orchestration

The default agent stays framework-neutral so the SDK does not hide native
MLflow or provider behavior. If the application needs durable checkpoints,
replay, or human approval, follow `recipes/langgraph/README.md`. The recipe
uses native LangGraph state APIs, injected persistent checkpoint/store
backends, an interrupt before side effects, and an application idempotency
key. `aai-core` continues to own resource context, trace policy, evaluation,
and release evidence rather than wrapping the graph.
